The baseline builds T_k with a recurrence, which costs 6 ciphertext-ciphertext multiplies and depth 8. I switch to a baby-step/giant-step split. Compute z, z², z³ and z⁴ (only 3 ciphertext multiplies, plus z⁴·B for a total of 4). Then evaluate y = (c0 + c1·z + c2·z² + c3·z³) + z⁴·(c4 + c5·z + c6·z² + c7·z³). This keeps the same 8 plaintext-coefficient multiplies, reduces ciphertext multiplies from 6 to 4, and lowers depth to about 5. The `one` input is no longer needed.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext alpha("alpha");
  Ciphertext z = x * alpha;

  Ciphertext z2 = z * z;
  Ciphertext z3 = z2 * z;
  Ciphertext z4 = z2 * z2;

  Plaintext c0("c0");
  Plaintext c1("c1");
  Plaintext c2("c2");
  Plaintext c3("c3");
  Plaintext c4("c4");
  Plaintext c5("c5");
  Plaintext c6("c6");
  Plaintext c7("c7");

  // low part: c0 + c1 z + c2 z^2 + c3 z^3
  Ciphertext lo = z * c1;
  Ciphertext t2 = z2 * c2;
  Ciphertext t3 = z3 * c3;
  lo = lo + t2;
  lo = lo + t3;
  lo = lo + c0;

  // high part: c4 + c5 z + c6 z^2 + c7 z^3
  Ciphertext hi = z * c5;
  Ciphertext u2 = z2 * c6;
  Ciphertext u3 = z3 * c7;
  hi = hi + u2;
  hi = hi + u3;
  hi = hi + c4;

  Ciphertext prod = z4 * hi;
  Ciphertext result = lo + prod;
  result.set_output("y");
}
```

The previous kernel used monomial powers z², z³ with Chebyshev coefficients, which is wrong. The coefficients c_k multiply T_k(z), not z^k. This version works in the Chebyshev basis.

I build T1 to T4 with the doubling identities T2 = 2z²−1, T3 = 2zT2−z and T4 = 2T2²−1. The degree-7 terms use T5 = 2T4·T1−T3, T6 = 2T4·T2−T2 and T7 = 2T4·T3−T1. So the sum becomes:

y = c0 + (c1−c7)T1 + (c2−c6)T2 + (c3−c5)T3 + c4·T4 + 2·T4·(c5·T1 + c6·T2 + c7·T3)

Each coefficient is applied as a separate ciphertext-plaintext multiply, since plaintext-plaintext subtraction is not allowed. This uses 4 ciphertext-ciphertext multiplies (T2, T3, T4, and T4·q) and depth 5.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext alpha("alpha");
  Plaintext one("one");
  Ciphertext z = x * alpha;

  // Chebyshev basis
  Ciphertext zz = z * z;
  Ciphertext T2 = zz + zz - one;
  Ciphertext zT2 = z * T2;
  Ciphertext T3 = zT2 + zT2 - z;
  Ciphertext T2s = T2 * T2;
  Ciphertext T4 = T2s + T2s - one;

  Plaintext c0("c0");
  Plaintext c1("c1");
  Plaintext c2("c2");
  Plaintext c3("c3");
  Plaintext c4("c4");
  Plaintext c5("c5");
  Plaintext c6("c6");
  Plaintext c7("c7");

  // low part: c0 + (c1-c7) T1 + (c2-c6) T2 + (c3-c5) T3 + c4 T4
  Ciphertext low = z * c1;
  Ciphertext a2 = T2 * c2;
  Ciphertext a3 = T3 * c3;
  Ciphertext a4 = T4 * c4;
  low = low + a2;
  low = low + a3;
  low = low + a4;
  low = low + c0;
  Ciphertext s1 = z * c7;
  Ciphertext s2 = T2 * c6;
  Ciphertext s3 = T3 * c5;
  low = low - s1;
  low = low - s2;
  low = low - s3;

  // high part: q = c5 T1 + c6 T2 + c7 T3, contributes 2 T4 q
  Ciphertext q1 = z * c5;
  Ciphertext q2 = T2 * c6;
  Ciphertext q3 = T3 * c7;
  Ciphertext q = q1 + q2;
  q = q + q3;
  Ciphertext P = T4 * q;
  Ciphertext P2 = P + P;

  Ciphertext result = low + P2;
  result.set_output("y");
}
```

The scale mismatch came from adding `c4·T4` into a level-4 sum whose other terms had a different multiplication history. This version removes that collision by folding `c4` into the product term:

- c4·T4 + 2·T4·q = T4·(2q + c4). So the kernel computes P = T4·(q+q+c4), where the plaintext `c4` is added to the ciphertext q+q before the multiply.
- Each group of terms that share a plaintext-multiply pattern is combined only with other ciphertexts from the same pattern (c1·z with c7·z, c2·T2 with c6·T2, c3·T3 with c5·T3). The groups are then added at strictly increasing levels, so no two same-level sums have different histories.
- c0 is added last, as the baseline does.

Math check: y = c0 + (c1−c7)T1 + (c2−c6)T2 + (c3−c5)T3 + T4·(2(c5T1 + c6T2 + c7T3) + c4). This uses 4 ciphertext multiplies and depth 5.

